{
  "8|DE-BW": {"anchors":["anchor-c649bced74ea4657ffc8"],"proofs":[["3ae66c6c2db6371a",23,"printed p.21, 3.2.2.2(6)"]],"scope":"Sek I Klassen 8/9/10 foundation, applicable to both subsequent technical course labels; no LK-only claim from selected 3.4.3(10).","sourceReason":"The original operation is performing and evaluating an acid/base titration. Actual liquid handling necessarily uses the prerequisite's basic goggles, clear/labelled workspace and stop/report rules. The alternative Sek-I anchor removes the selected LF-only limitation."},
  "8|DE-BB": {"anchors":["anchor-7300a0c3e0bbab4c2180"],"proofs":[["2fa91c684e7a8735",45,"p.45, 3.2.6, GK baseline plus LK"]],"scope":"Qualifikationsphase GK baseline and LK; neutral-equivalence strong-acid/base experiment. Multi-proton/curve additions remain LK-specific.","sourceReason":"Original p.45 explicitly includes a strong-acid/base titration as an experiment in the GK column, which LK builds on. Its actual safe performance needs the basic safety competence. Source-cell p.44 is corrected to the actual operation page p.45."},
  "8|DE-BE": {"anchors":["anchor-c8bfc3dd704392fd3e88"],"proofs":[["2fa91c684e7a8735",45,"p.45, 3.2.6, GK baseline plus LK"]],"scope":"Qualifikationsphase GK baseline and LK; strong-acid/base experiment. Curve/multi-proton additions remain LK-specific.","sourceReason":"The common BB/BE original p.45 explicitly requires the experimental titration in the GK baseline. Safe handling requires the basic safety competence. The supplied anchor's p.44 locator does not identify the operation page; p.45 does."},
  "8|DE-NI": {"anchors":["anchor-b0270460cc3fe7a7a24e"],"proofs":[["bd082d15919b9a6c",21,"p.21 Protonenübertragungsreaktionen, E column"]],"scope":"Qualifikationsphase gA/eA common operation, unlike eA-only pH-curve recording.","sourceReason":"The alternative original operation is determining acid/base concentration by titration, not merely describing an indicator. Actually carrying out this measurement necessarily needs basic safe workspace, eye protection and stopping/reporting on an unclear vessel or splash."},
  "12|DE-NW": {"anchors":["anchor-4abdbe9913c8ff4f91cf","anchor-8d5d5ec3622249b267ed"],"proofs":[["24af8d883b2b6090",39,"actual p.39, GK titration performance/evaluation"],["24af8d883b2b6090",48,"actual p.48, LK titration performance/evaluation"]],"scope":"Separate actual GK and LK source operations retained; the supplied extraction labels them pp.38/47, whereas this exact PDF prints pp.39/48.","sourceReason":"Both original course rows require performing titration and evaluating its results including errors. Quantitative concentration determination requires the meaning of named analytical amounts in mol and stoichiometric entity ratios; this is not supplied by just knowing an indicator colour."},
  "12|DE-SH": {"anchors":["anchor-53a80a1ad8b1903b3d23"],"proofs":[["ab56f18400f42799",65,"p.65 Analytik, gAN/eAN common left column"]],"scope":"Both levels; analytic content is taught within the chosen mandatory water-or-soil environmental area. No selection of both environmental areas is inferred.","sourceReason":"The original common-column operation explicitly joins acid/base titration with concentration calculation and lists amounts/concentrations. A meaningful aliquot/stoichiometry calculation needs the mol/entity competence. The chosen alternative avoids using LK-only titration curves as universal support."},
  "12|DE-HH": {"anchors":["anchor-525dfbf432e59eee281b"],"proofs":[["4f452d282fdef5f2",30,"p.30, 2.4, common acid/base titration concentration calculation"]],"scope":"Studienstufe gA and eA common strong-acid/base concentration calculation; weak/multi-proton curves remain eA additions.","sourceReason":"The actual common-level original lists strong-acid/base endpoint concentration determination and concentration calculations. Those require amount, mol and reaction-entity ratios. Amino-acid buffering, the mechanically selected source, is replaced by the directly relevant titration operation."},
  "12|DE-MV": {"anchors":["anchor-5fe92aa586d0d84acf53"],"proofs":[["dcc123a0291510ae",28,"PDF p.28 / printed p.24, Säure-Base-Titration"]],"scope":"Qualification GK/LK common content of the exact Erprobungsfassung; additional LK titration modes are not universalized.","sourceReason":"The original common titration block requires principle and evaluation of acid/base titration and calculations of concentrations, amounts and masses. These require the mol/entity meaning and stoichiometric amount relationships. The unrelated selected redox-titration anchor is replaced."},
  "12|DE-ST": {"anchors":["anchor-750a850b746ecff911a7","anchor-3b9b17522438e4ec3a74"],"proofs":[["fcdbb0bdaf4ccad6",40,"p.40 gAN 11/12"],["fcdbb0bdaf4ccad6",49,"p.49 eAN 11/12"]],"scope":"gAN and eAN supported separately; the distinct Wahlpflichtfach p.58 is not used as a GK substitute.","sourceReason":"Both actual 11/12 course rows require using acid/base titration as a quantitative method and concentration calculations. This requires named amounts in mol and stoichiometric relationships. A pKB knowledge bullet alone would not establish this; the two direct titration anchors do."},
  "12|DE-TH": {"anchors":["anchor-18db64c531e5c3c7de79"],"proofs":[["f89768dabb9f273d",51,"PDF p.51 / printed p.46, common left column"]],"scope":"Qualification basic and increased levels share strong-acid/base titration and calculation; more advanced curve/method requirements stay in the added right column.","sourceReason":"The original explicitly demands explaining quantitative titration and calculating analytical concentration or mass fraction. That calculation cannot be understood without what a mol of the specified entities means and how reaction ratios change analytical amount."},
  "13|DE-BW": {"anchors":["anchor-e3ed5a1c546657413368"],"proofs":[["3ae66c6c2db6371a",23,"PDF p.23 / printed p.21, 3.2.2.2(7)"]],"scope":"Sek I Klassen 8/9/10 quantitative foundation; current docket target is the technical GK view. No extra official LK exclusivity.","sourceReason":"The original explicitly includes molar-volume calculations with correct physical quantities/units. Justifying a common ideal-gas Vm under fixed T,p requires the equal-volume/equal-particle-amount relation and its condition boundary. It supports acquisition, not a claim that the official row explicitly names Avogadro."},
  "29|DE-NI": {"anchors":["anchor-115d869e701d8f743bcc"],"proofs":[["bd082d15919b9a6c",28,"p.28 Bewertung, common gA/eA safety"]],"scope":"Common qualification gA/eA laboratory/everyday risk context, not an eA-only mechanism or a separate practical program.","sourceReason":"The original assessment of hazards and safety in laboratory/everyday settings requires judging whether protections match the substance/activity and exposure. Justifying protection against the actual splash or inhalation route is necessary to a reasoned safety judgment, rather than just decoding a pictogram."},
  "35|DE-NI": {"anchors":["anchor-1aa782345c5f87eba63c"],"proofs":[["bd082d15919b9a6c",13,"p.13 EP Reaktionen von Alkanen"]],"scope":"Einführungsphase common foundation for later gA/eA; the comparison concerns fossil versus renewable feedstocks with equal function/system boundaries.","sourceReason":"The original sustainability comparison and adjacent evidence-grounded communication/research rows require selecting and structuring chemically relevant claims. In the current actual research tasks the author/date/claim must be recorded for a bounded comparison; the source-information competence is necessary to that acquisition route."},
  "35|DE-ST": {"anchors":["anchor-03a54d50d4aba9806643"],"proofs":[["fcdbb0bdaf4ccad6",30,"p.30 media research, critical check, documentation"],["fcdbb0bdaf4ccad6",31,"actual p.31 sustainability evaluation, continuation of topic"]],"scope":"Schuljahrgang 9 foundation, common to subsequent course labels; no course-specific advanced demand inferred.","sourceReason":"The original topic requires targeted media research, critical information checking and documentation on p.30, followed by ecological/economic/social evaluation of natural gas, oil and biogas on p.31. That combination genuinely requires the prerequisite's question-relevant selection, structured interpretation and source attribution. The supplied evaluation locator p.30 is corrected to p.31."},
  "38|DE-NI": {"anchors":["anchor-379c6bfd8f9650e63682","anchor-96f922c6321b48534f1b"],"proofs":[["bd082d15919b9a6c",26,"p.26 common local elements, corrosion, sacrificial anode and protection experiments"]],"scope":"Qualification common gA/eA corrosion/protection; eA coordinate bonding on the same page is a separate additional requirement.","sourceReason":"The actual source explicitly applies galvanic knowledge to local elements, explains sacrificial-anode protection and conducts protection experiments. These operations require the prior local-corrosion, protection and sacrificial-anode competence. The selected oxygen/acid distinction alone would have been too narrow; the direct protection alternatives supply the needed operation."},
  "42|DE-NI": {"anchors":["anchor-8af6c96316d401252b6c","anchor-6039d71defbe247feebc"],"proofs":[["bd082d15919b9a6c",26,"p.26 common electrolysis principle and galvanic comparison"]],"scope":"Common qualification gA/eA; Faraday quantitative equations and overvoltage remain eA-only.","sourceReason":"The original electrolysis-principle explanation and comparison with a galvanic cell require forced ionic/electrode processes and energy direction, not just an apparatus sketch. The simple-salt process competence is necessary to the current whole explanation and its stated aqueous/melt boundary."},
  "42|DE-SH": {"anchors":["anchor-036b2f3e6b0e011387f8"],"proofs":[["ab56f18400f42799",67,"p.67 common gAN/eAN Redoxreaktionen und Stromgewinnung"]],"scope":"Both levels share electrochemical material production as forced redox; Faraday laws and overvoltage are eAN additions.","sourceReason":"The original common operation explicitly explains electrolysis as forced redox for producing substances. Ion migration and coupled metal/nonmetal electrode processes are necessary to a material-level explanation of that process; the current dependent adds product ratios and the galvanic comparison."},
  "68|DE-BW": {"anchors":["anchor-1bf151a87b2bc32b99a4"],"proofs":[["3ae66c6c2db6371a",23,"PDF p.23 / printed p.21, 3.2.2.2(3)-(5)"]],"scope":"Sek I Klassen 8/9/10 foundation, common to both later technical course labels; the separate full redox competence is not inferred as official content here.","sourceReason":"The original demands writing equations in formula notation and, on the same page, forming/interpreting molecular formulas. Symbol interpretation, atom counts and index/coefficient distinction are necessary to writing a chemically invariant balanced equation. This is an indirect acquisition support finding, not direct source coverage of every redox task."},
  "68|DE-HH": {"anchors":["anchor-1f49628d71d08a1afc67"],"proofs":[["4f452d282fdef5f2",31,"p.31 common redox partial reactions/equations with oxidation numbers"]],"scope":"Studienstufe common gA/eA block; the explicitly added lithium-ion accumulator/Nernst details are not part of this support claim.","sourceReason":"The original common block requires partial electron-transfer reactions and reaction equations with oxidation numbers. Correct symbols/formulas, atom counts and coefficient versus index meaning are necessary to that operation. The source does not become an explicit quotation demanding the precursor goal."},
  "68|DE-RP": {"anchors":["anchor-0a15e169c67dd860c25a"],"proofs":[["307f5d841072a1a3",78,"PDF p.78 / printed p.76, TF6 Säuren und Laugen"]],"scope":"Sek I TF6 foundation; later technical GK/LK share the same prerequisite. No LK-only pharmacology or separate program route is used.","sourceReason":"The original TF6 competencies explicitly explain neutralisation in formula language, and the conceptual row describes neutralisation with reaction equations. Interpreting/forming correct formulas and distinguishing indices from coefficients is necessary to that symbolic explanation. PDF p.78 and printed p.76 are distinguished."},
  "69|DE-NW": {"anchors":["anchor-9761cbf34d8fc1dc5e92","anchor-3b8b48a791ddb7981f74"],"proofs":[["24af8d883b2b6090",38,"actual p.38 GK Brønsted classification with protolysis equations"],["24af8d883b2b6090",47,"actual p.47 LK counterpart"]],"scope":"Separate actual GK and LK Brønsted/protolysis operations; the original source's gA/LK wording remains distinct from the old irrelevant element-family anchor.","sourceReason":"Both original course rows explicitly require describing Brønsted behaviour with protolysis equations. This needs correct molecular/ionic symbolic composition and atom/proton accounting. The unrelated mechanically selected PSE-property prediction is replaced by direct same-endpoint Brønsted anchors."},
  "71|DE-BW": {"anchors":["anchor-30bcd3e0d500dea3ee51","anchor-659547ea7fa2d0a8657a"],"proofs":[["3ae66c6c2db6371a",29,"PDF p.29 / printed p.27, BF 3.3.3(5)"],["3ae66c6c2db6371a",38,"PDF p.38 / printed p.36, LF 3.4.4(16)"]],"scope":"BF requires carbohydrate/protein investigation including Biuret; LF adds the explicit amino-acid/protein Ninhydrin+Biuret operation. Generic guided-investigation support is common, but Ninhydrin is not declared a BF duty.","sourceReason":"The original BF and LF rows require carrying out appropriate substance tests, and the LF row expressly says perform and describe Ninhydrin/Biuret. Actual safe controlled performance and own records require guided-investigation competence. Two anchors preserve the different course operations instead of universalising the selected LF amino-acid test."},
  "72|DE-NI": {"anchors":["anchor-0de1d5f4ad5d56522dd6"],"proofs":[["bd082d15919b9a6c",32,"p.32 E column, actual Iod-Stärke-Reaktion"]],"scope":"Common gA/eA starch experiment. The same page's eA-only Biuret/other protein additions remain separate; no universal Vitamin-C or complete food-panel official duty is inferred.","sourceReason":"The source explicitly requires actually performing the iodine-starch reaction. A meaningful controlled test in the current food investigation requires safe guided steps, own honest observation and linking the test to its starch proposition. The support is for acquisition of that actual branch, not full official coverage of all authored food tests."}
